# 6. Build the Application

Open a terminal in the BankCRUD project folder.

Run:

```powershell
mvn clean package -DskipTests
```

### What this does

* Cleans the previous build
* Compiles the Java code
* Packages the application
* Creates the Spring Boot JAR file

The JAR will be created under:

```text
target/
```

---

# 7. Check Docker Containers

Run:

```powershell
docker ps
```

This displays currently running Docker containers.

After BankCRUD is started, you should see containers similar to:

```text
bankcrud-app
bankcrud-mysql
```

---

# 8. Start BankCRUD with Docker

Run:

```powershell
docker compose up --build
```

### What this does

Docker Compose starts:

1. MySQL container
2. Spring Boot application container

The `--build` option tells Docker to build the BankCRUD application image using the Dockerfile.

---

# 9. Access the Application

Open your browser:

```text
http://localhost:8099/
```

This opens the static BankCRUD UI.

From the UI you can:

* Add account
* View accounts
* Edit account
* Delete account

---

# 10. Access MySQL

The MySQL container is available through host port **3309**.

Run:

```powershell
docker exec -it bankcrud-mysql mysql -u root -p
```

When prompted for the password, enter:

```text
root
```

---

# 11. Check the Database

After entering MySQL:

```sql
SHOW DATABASES;
```

Select the BankCRUD database:

```sql
USE bankcrud;
```

Show tables:

```sql
SHOW TABLES;
```

View bank accounts:

```sql
SELECT * FROM bank_account;
```

---

# 12. Docker Ports

| Component       |   Port |
| --------------- | -----: |
| Spring Boot     | `8099` |
| MySQL Host      | `3309` |
| MySQL Container | `3306` |

The browser uses:

```text
localhost:8099
```

MySQL is accessed from the Windows host through:

```text
localhost:3309
```

Inside Docker, Spring Boot connects to MySQL using:

```text
mysql:3306
```

The reason is that `mysql` is the Docker Compose service name.

---

# 13. Stop the Application

To stop the containers:

```powershell
docker compose down
```

This stops and removes the containers but keeps the MySQL data volume.

---

# 14. Remove Containers and Database Data

If you want to completely remove the containers and MySQL volume:

```powershell
docker compose down -v
```

**Warning:** This deletes the stored MySQL data for this Docker Compose project.

